i='TCS-3788';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Word-RAM conventions in Section 1; summary items (3)–(4); Section 2.3 ordered Δ-partition and splitter variant; Section 2.5 and conclusion ask about randomization.'
refs += [dict(id='partition',title='A Linear Time Algorithm for Ordered Partition',authors='Yijie Han',year=2015,url='https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-19647-3_9',pdf_url='https://yupinglu.me/files/FAW2015.pdf',locator='FAW 2015, LNCS 9130, pages 89–103; linear-time deterministic partition into Θ(√n) buckets of size Θ(√n), not arbitrary bucket counts; predates the question.')]
issue='The source asks broadly whether randomization speeds up ordered partitioning, but does not specify a new running-time bound, joint regime of n, word size w and bucket size Δ, or randomized correctness and time convention. Literal improvement somewhere over its deterministic partition bound is already supplied at Δ=1 by randomized sorting results that the same paper cites. Thus a complete intended question must identify what remains to improve beyond those known bounds—for example an explicit parameter regime and target cost—without inventing it. The dynamic consequence also needs an operation set, update/query cost tradeoff and adaptive-adversary convention. No particular stronger theorem is selected by the source.'
notes=['Recovered ordered integer partitioning as the actual primitive, rather than a generic data-structure speedup.','Defined word size, bucket ordering, the distinction between balanced output buckets and supplied splitters, and the approximate-heap consequence.','Restored the deterministic n,w,Δ bound with a linear-time floor and separated exact sorting and soft heaps.','Noted that the source already cites randomized sorting speedups at Δ=1, so the literal existential question is not a new open proposition.','Checked the older 2015 linear partition theorem and later searches; it only treats square-root-size buckets and is not a new general resolution.']
save(i,dict(title='Randomized complexity of ordered integer partitioning',formal='The source asks whether randomization can further accelerate ordered partitioning of word-sized integer keys and thereby improve approximate heap operations. It studies n keys, word length w and target bucket size Δ, with a deterministic upper bound O(n·max{1,log₂(log₂n/log₂(wΔ))}) in the nontrivial range, interpreting the expression as linear time whenever the ratio inside the outer logarithm is at most a constant. The intended new randomized bound and parameter regime are not stated. Since Δ=1 is exact sorting and the source already cites randomized sorting speedups, the bare assertion “randomization improves this deterministic bound for some parameters” is not certified as a new open question.',definitions='In the source’s word-RAM model, a memory cell holds w bits and a key fits in one word, with w≥⌈log₂(n+1)⌉ so item positions can be addressed. Standard arithmetic, comparisons, bitwise operations, shifts and indexed memory access on a constant number of words cost one operation; arbitrary-precision integers do not have unit cost. An ordered partition of a collection A of n keyed items is a list of disjoint buckets A₁,…,A_b whose union is A and such that every key in A_i is at most every key in A_j for i<j. Items need not be ordered inside a bucket, and repeated keys cause no difficulty in this non-strict ordering convention. The balanced Δ-partition version has b=Θ(n/Δ) and buckets of size Θ(Δ), allowing boundary rounding, for 1≤Δ≤n. At Δ=1 this means sorting, up to constant-size buckets that can be sorted in linear additional time. A second version takes sorted splitters s₁<⋯<s_b and assigns each item to its interval between consecutive splitters; interval buckets may be highly unbalanced. The source relates these static problems for its asymptotic equivalences but they are not identical output requirements. An approximate Δ-min query returns one of the Δ smallest stored items. An approximate heap supports insertion, deletion and Δ-min; its parameter is an additive rank tolerance, not multiplicative key error. A general approximate select query instead receives a target rank and returns an item whose rank differs by less than Δ. The source establishes different bounds for those operations. A soft heap uses yet another convention, allowing a bounded fraction of keys to be increased, and its equivalence is to exact sorting at a smaller size. The source’s data structures use linear space; reported amortized bounds can be made worst-case by cited methods. For a new randomized target, Las Vegas correctness (always correct, expected time) and Monte Carlo correctness (bounded error with a time bound), as well as worst-case versus expected time, must be distinguished. None is chosen for the broad concluding question.',question_type='asymptotic_complexity',answer_criterion='Specify a source-supported unresolved randomized ordered-partition target, including the exact input/output version, n,w,Δ regime, randomness/error convention, space and requested time improvement over all applicable known bounds. Then establish that improvement or matching lower bounds at the required precision. Merely quoting the existing randomized sorting algorithm, the linear square-root-bucket partition theorem, or a result for approximate select instead of approximate min does not complete the intended new tradeoff. The remaining target is not yet fully specified.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Partitioning connects to approximate minimum queries',text='The source relates partition time per item to approximate-heap operation time. It separates this from general approximate select, for which it gives matching cell-probe bounds. The explicit consequence discussed in the introduction is the possibility of faster approximate min for smaller Δ.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Some regimes are already linear',text='A deterministic linear-time primitive partitions into roughly √n buckets of roughly √n items. Iterating such primitives gives the broader deterministic bound. Han’s 2015 paper presents this square-root partition result; it is not a linear algorithm for arbitrary Δ.',citation='partition'),dict(title='Sorting speedups are already part of the source context',text='The source cites randomized O(n√log log n) sorting and linear expected sorting for sufficiently long words. Sorting can also produce ordered buckets. Any new randomized partition question must account for those existing upper bounds, especially at Δ=1.',citation='primary'),dict(title='A dynamic consequence adds further conventions',text='Passing a randomized primitive to a dynamic structure requires a guarantee for the whole operation sequence. Expected amortized cost, per-operation worst-case cost and the adversary’s access to past answers are different promises; the source does not select a new randomized version.',citation='primary')],why='Ordered partitioning captures how much order information is needed for approximate priority queues, potentially allowing improvements that are invisible when one insists on fully sorting every item. Its meaningful randomized tradeoff must go beyond already-known sorting speedups.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2015',text='A deterministic linear-time square-root-bucket partition algorithm is presented, predating this question.',citation='partition'),dict(date='2019',text='The source relates partitioning to approximate heaps, gives parameter-dependent deterministic bounds, and asks broadly about further benefits of randomization.',citation='primary')],status='uncertain',status_note='Read the source’s models, static/dynamic equivalences, deterministic bound and both randomization passages. Checked the 2015 partition result against its exact bucket count; later searches through 12 September 2026 did not identify a source fixing the new target intended here. The formulation must distinguish already-known randomized sorting speedups from an unresolved improvement for ordered partitioning.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,statement_review=dict(status='needs_specification',reviewed_on=DATE,notes=notes,remaining_issue=issue),review_note=issue,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A structural relation between partial ordering and approximate priority-queue complexity, but the desired randomized improvement requires an explicit parameter regime and guarantee. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['Ordered partitioning places word-sized keys into ordered buckets without sorting inside each bucket.','The source relates the cost per item to approximate-minimum heap operations.','It asks whether randomization gives further improvements over its deterministic bounds.','Randomized sorting and linear-time square-root-bucket partitioning are already known and do not identify the intended remaining target.','The card recovers the primitive and parameters while leaving the new bound, parameter regime and randomized guarantee to be specified.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','partition'])),notes=notes,checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://yupinglu.me/files/FAW2015.pdf'],state='improved_unfinished')
